{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>He brought out bread and wine.</b> According to the plain flow, it should have written 'and the king of Sodom said to Abram' immediately after 'and the king of Sodom went out to meet him.' But because Abram was about to say 'from a thread to a shoelace,' the hearers would say: if by Abraham's word, from what do he and all his men-at-arms make a living? Therefore he interrupted and wrote in the meantime 'and Malki-Tzedek king of Shalem brought out bread and wine' — to teach us that Abraham spoke truth to the king of Sodom: 'if I take from all that is yours' — yet he did benefit from that of Malki-Tzedek. Another interpretation: <b>and Malki-Tzedek brought out bread and wine</b> — they were tevel (untithed produce), so that Abraham might separate tithe from them, for we find that Abraham was a priest, as explained."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:04:13.638231774Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}